\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Catalyst Resolution Enhancement (Theorem~6.5). (a)~Progressive resolution improvement on a logarithmic scale as each of 12 biological catalysts is applied sequentially; resolution decreases from the diffraction limit $\Delta x_0 = 200\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm  {nm}$ to $\Delta x_{12} = 5.06 \times 10^{-9}\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm  {nm}$ after all catalysts, confirming the multiplicative law $\Delta x_k = \Delta x_{k-1} \cdot \varepsilon _k$. Forward, reverse, and random application orders yield identical final resolutions (difference $< 10^{-24}\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm  {nm}$), validating order independence (Theorem~6.5). (b)~Exclusion factors $\varepsilon _i$ for each catalyst; O$_2$ triangulation provides the strongest constraint ($\varepsilon = 0.05$), while mass conservation and cytoskeletal phase-lock are weakest ($\varepsilon = 0.25$ and $0.20$). (c)~Three-dimensional resolution surface over (catalyst index, modality index), showing the combined effect of sequential catalysis and multi-modal fusion; the surface drops monotonically in both dimensions, confirming that additional constraints and modalities both reduce resolution independently. (d)~Final resolution comparison on logarithmic scale: diffraction limit ($200\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm  {nm}$), after catalysis alone ($5.06 \times 10^{-9}\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm  {nm}$), and after fusion with $\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _{j<k} \rho _{jk} = 4.05$ ($8.82 \times 10^{-11}\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm  {nm}$); total enhancement factor $2.27 \times 10^{12}$.}}{12}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:panel_exp2_purpose}{{3}{12}{Catalyst Resolution Enhancement (Theorem~6.5). (a)~Progressive resolution improvement on a logarithmic scale as each of 12 biological catalysts is applied sequentially; resolution decreases from the diffraction limit $\Delta x_0 = 200\;\mathrm {nm}$ to $\Delta x_{12} = 5.06 \times 10^{-9}\;\mathrm {nm}$ after all catalysts, confirming the multiplicative law $\Delta x_k = \Delta x_{k-1} \cdot \varepsilon _k$. Forward, reverse, and random application orders yield identical final resolutions (difference $< 10^{-24}\;\mathrm {nm}$), validating order independence (Theorem~6.5). (b)~Exclusion factors $\varepsilon _i$ for each catalyst; O$_2$ triangulation provides the strongest constraint ($\varepsilon = 0.05$), while mass conservation and cytoskeletal phase-lock are weakest ($\varepsilon = 0.25$ and $0.20$). (c)~Three-dimensional resolution surface over (catalyst index, modality index), showing the combined effect of sequential catalysis and multi-modal fusion; the surface drops monotonically in both dimensions, confirming that additional constraints and modalities both reduce resolution independently. (d)~Final resolution comparison on logarithmic scale: diffraction limit ($200\;\mathrm {nm}$), after catalysis alone ($5.06 \times 10^{-9}\;\mathrm {nm}$), and after fusion with $\sum _{j<k} \rho _{jk} = 4.05$ ($8.82 \times 10^{-11}\;\mathrm {nm}$); total enhancement factor $2.27 \times 10^{12}$}{figure.3}{}}
